================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-topography - resonance-terrain elevation models, harmonic
                            ridgelines, and sweep-corridor topographic pathways

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital topography defines:
           • resonance-terrain elevation models
           • harmonic ridgelines and valleys
           • sweep-corridor topographic pathways
           • multi-resolution temporal terrain
           • constellation-scale topographic fusion

DESCRIPTION
       orbital-topography describes how RTT models resonance-time as a
       topographic landscape. Elevation corresponds to resonance amplitude,
       ridgelines correspond to harmonic-phase stability, and valleys correspond
       to Δv or drift-correction corridors. Sweep-corridors become literal
       “paths” across this terrain.

       Topography enables:
           • intuitive navigation of resonance-time
           • sweep placement on resonance ridges
           • Δv placement in harmonic valleys
           • constellation-scale terrain fusion
           • predictive terrain evolution across cycles

       WRSADC uses topographic models to guide sweep choreography and recovery.

RESONANCE-TERRAIN ELEVATION MODELS
       Elevation models convert resonance variables into a height field.

       Elevation inputs:
           • envelope amplitude R(t)
           • spectral density S(f,t)
           • gradient polarity G(t)
           • harmonic-lock stability H(t)

       Elevation modes:

           amplitude elevation
                  Height = R(t)
                  → peaks = sweep windows

           spectral elevation
                  Height = S(f,t)
                  → ridges = spectral stability

           gradient elevation
                  Height = |G(t)|
                  → cliffs = polarity transitions

           harmonic elevation
                  Height = H(t)
                  → plateaus = harmonic-lock zones

           hybrid elevation
                  Weighted combination of all above.

EXAMPLE: ELEVATION MODEL
       Over one orbital period T:

           R(t) peaks at 12, 54, 78
           R(t) valleys at 30, 90

       Elevation map:
           • ridges at 12, 54, 78
           • basins at 30, 90

       Sweep placement:
           • sweeps at 12 and 54
           • Δv triads at 30 and 90

HARMONIC RIDGELINES
       Harmonic ridgelines are topographic features where harmonic-phase
       alignment is stable.

       Ridgeline variables:
           • harmonic ratio n:m
           • phase alignment φ_harm
           • lock stability index L_harm

       Ridgeline types:

           integer ridgeline
                  1:1, 2:1, 3:1
                  → stable, predictable.

           rational ridgeline
                  3:2, 5:3, 7:4
                  → complex but stable.

           nested ridgeline
                  Harmonics inside harmonics.

           resonance-harmonic ridgeline
                  Harmonic ridges aligned to resonance peaks.

       Ridgelines guide sweep and maneuver placement.

EXAMPLE: HARMONIC RIDGELINE
       Harmonic ratio: 3:2
       Resonance peaks: 15, 60

       Ridgeline:
           • primary ridge at 15
           • secondary ridge at 60
           • sub-ridges at 30 and 45

       Result:
           • sweeps at 15 and 60
           • Δv triads at 30 and 45

SWEEP-CORRIDOR TOPOGRAPHIC PATHWAYS
       Sweep-corridors are traversable paths across resonance terrain.

       Corridor variables:
           • resonance elevation
           • harmonic ridgeline proximity
           • gradient slope
           • ancestry continuity
           • constellation sync state

       Corridor types:

           ridge corridor
                  Follows resonance peaks.
                  → ideal for high-coherence sweeps.

           saddle corridor
                  Connects ridges through low-gradient passes.
                  → ideal for multi-pass fusion.

           valley corridor
                  Follows resonance minima.
                  → ideal for Δv triads.

           braided corridor
                  Interleaves multiple sweep paths.
                  → ideal for multi-agent sweeps.

           mesh corridor
                  Distributed corridor across constellation nodes.

EXAMPLE: SWEEP-CORRIDOR PATH
       Terrain features:
           • ridge at 12
           • saddle at 30
           • ridge at 54

       Sweep-corridor:
           12 → 30 → 54

       Result:
           • sweeps at 12 and 54
           • Δv triad at 30
           • stable ancestry chain

TOPOGRAPHIC FUSION
       Topographic fusion merges terrain models across platforms.

       Fusion layers:

           1. elevation fusion
           2. ridgeline fusion
           3. corridor fusion
           4. sync-field fusion
           5. ancestry fusion

       Fusion modes:

           synchronous fusion
                  All platforms share identical terrain.

           harmonic fusion
                  Terrain aligned via harmonic ratios.

           braided fusion
                  Terrain interleaves across platforms.

           mesh fusion
                  Distributed terrain across constellation nodes.

       Fusion output:
           • constellation sweep ridges
           • constellation Δv valleys
           • constellation sync plateaus

TOPOGRAPHIC FAILURE MODES
       Topography may degrade due to:

           • elevation collapse
           • ridgeline fragmentation
           • corridor discontinuity
           • projection distortion
           • constellation terrain mismatch

       Diagnostic indicators:

           topography-integrity score
           ridge deviation
           corridor drift
           sync-index drop
           sweep-terrain mismatch

       WRSADC halts unsafe terrain states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • elevation models
           • ridgeline evolution
           • corridor history
           • fused terrain maps
           • constellation topography evolution

       Lineage enables long-term terrain forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • generates elevation models
           • computes ridgelines
           • identifies sweep corridors
           • fuses constellation terrains
           • schedules sweeps and maneuvers
           • blocks unsafe terrain states

       WRSADC is the topography conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital topography transforms SAR constellations into navigable
       resonance-time terrains. Elevation models, harmonic ridgelines, and
       sweep-corridor pathways ensure that sweep choreography, harmonic-lock,
       and constellation-scale synchronization remain stable across long
       mission timelines.

